#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125282} :activity/id #uuid "4335d62f-6a14-5864-be7f-c04874251357" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125282 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125729 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $f(x,y) = x y^2 - x^3 y.$ Then the first-order partial derivatives are $$\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} (x,y) = y^2 -3x^2y \\quad \\mathrm{and} \\quad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x,y) = 2xy -x^3,$$\n\nand the second-order **mixed partial derivatives** are $$\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial y\\, \\partial x}(x,y) = 2y -3x^2 \\quad \\mathrm{and} \\quad \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x\\, \\partial y}(x,y) = 2y-3x^2.$$\n\nNotice that these two mixed partial derivatives are equal, i.e., $$\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial y\\, \\partial x}=\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x\\, \\partial y}.$$\n\nIn general, if a function $f(x,y)$ has continuous first-order partial derivatives on an open set $U,$ and the second-order mixed partial derivatives exist and are continuous on $U,$ then the mixed partial derivatives are equal everywhere on $U.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "96f3873b-8f1b-5481-827e-503e557bbe9c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2408 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "81952bcf-8d7d-50aa-9c3d-0f75127d5ba3" :step/math-academy-id 10705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125283}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125283 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124448 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124252 :question/id #uuid "2247dc40-0c71-596c-9da4-a3bffd85e106" :question/math-academy-id "e-3152"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ee40fd-e452-5593-9a35-7c46d1f0cca0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048781}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404121}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Constants in First-Order Partial Derivative Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "3cac5a52-91a4-530b-93a7-3b880c3d7869" :step/math-academy-id 10709 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125285}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125284 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124449 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124253 :question/id #uuid "d45926a5-0126-50b4-bdc7-a9f686408986" :question/math-academy-id "e-3153"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7619454-7a2b-5c0b-927c-24dca2e564c3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409651}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Expressions of Partial Derivatives at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "03eb0268-bd36-5a13-83fe-9c238bbe818f" :step/math-academy-id 10710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125285 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124254 :question/id #uuid "d62e8502-b3c5-5923-8ab3-ef988507cd2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3154"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394064}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "e1813dc3-f991-54bc-bf74-b3e674f858b0" :step/math-academy-id 10713 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125330}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125330 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125755 :tutorial/content "If $f(x,y)$ is a function whose first-order and second-order mixed partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ then the second-order mixed partial derivatives are equal everywhere on $U.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x\\, \\partial y} =\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial y\\, \\partial x}\n$$\n\nThis result also extends to higher-order partial derivatives. For example, if the third-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)$ are also continuous everywhere on $U,$ then the third-order mixed partial derivatives are independent of the order in which the differentiation is performed. \n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{blue}\\partial x\\, \\partial x\\, \\partial y} =\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{red}\\partial y\\, \\partial x \\, \\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can think of $\\color{blue}\\partial x\\, \\partial x\\, \\partial y$ and $\\color{red}\\partial y\\, \\partial x \\, \\partial x$ as distinct permutations of the symbols $\\partial x,\\partial x,$ and $\\partial y.$ Any permutation of these symbols gives the same result. For example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{blue}\\partial x\\, \\partial x\\, \\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{purple}\\partial x\\, \\partial y\\, \\partial x}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** This does **not** mean that *all* third partial derivatives are equal! For example, it is generally true that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\color{blue}\\partial x\\, \\partial x\\, \\partial y} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial^3 f}{\\partial x\\, \\partial y\\, \\partial y}.\n$$\n\n \nThese are not equal because $\\partial x\\, \\partial y\\, \\partial y$ (in the right-hand side denominator) is a permutation of the symbols $\\partial x, \\partial y, \\partial y,$ not $\\partial x, \\partial x, \\partial y.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2267ab49-4a4d-553c-ba46-127edac4a2e8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2884 :tutorial/title "Equality of Higher-Order Partial Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "fec110b5-a92e-591e-b9da-4dceefec8548" :step/math-academy-id 15480 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125284}}] :activity/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124742}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409654} :activity/id #uuid "3c715a18-b04f-5fa8-b565-cd1d87a53bb9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4421a8c6-aea6-5d62-9cd0-8a06f8915dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409638} {:answer/id #uuid "45730328-f0dc-5c33-a6c2-53d71d04445d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409639} {:answer/id #uuid "4d58c242-f6fd-5d67-8e96-0273fcae35a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409641} {:answer/id #uuid "59fd5070-c975-5965-b9d4-d62ba5834853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409642} {:answer/id #uuid "d26ca855-4a28-50fd-bba1-040fec87c89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409642} :answer-field/id #uuid "504a276b-9fed-5959-a86c-c2b987992901" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "923e8280-3481-5e90-8081-225a78ef2cea" :question/math-academy-id "q-133393" :question/problem "Evaluate ${f}_{zxz}(2,\\frac{1}{2},1)$ given that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n{f}_{zz}(x,y,z)=2z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+x{e}^{z-2y}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zzx}(x,y,z) & ={f}_{zxz}(x,y,z) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{zz}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2z\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y)+x{e}^{z-2y}) \\\\  & =2z\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2y}+{e}^{z-2y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2z}{x-2y}+{e}^{z-2y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point  $(2,\\frac{1}{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zxz}(2,\\frac{1}{2},1) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}+{e}^{1-2\\cdot 1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-1}+{e}^{1-1} \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "035a465c-a1c6-5467-ae17-a4aa635d3e19"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "073fdb5d-0bf0-53ec-9b05-581d48c4f32d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345415} {:answer/id #uuid "1278b104-5c6b-5747-a4cb-f788b7ad603e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345510} {:answer/id #uuid "19da4515-ce77-55fb-8320-4d341a02db44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345577} {:answer/id #uuid "36144c74-e587-583a-b129-66bce59d2bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345818} {:answer/id #uuid "d096879f-ad55-5525-8639-33cc1186b3f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347179}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345818} :answer-field/id #uuid "5de9ecf8-1b93-55b8-b084-fe8277d7e5e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0fedf8ed-0c48-5105-b489-f0efda76d273" :question/math-academy-id "q-80759" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{-2x}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{-y}-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=5$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-2x}-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-y}-2x)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2})=4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy) \\\\  & =2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."} :step/id #uuid "55584015-c136-5819-9111-6b2b98328a75" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "103022cc-3fb6-5c83-9687-f61b42ee3fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321274} {:answer/id #uuid "21d76b19-bc18-56ad-9884-c518f507ceb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321401} {:answer/id #uuid "55275e49-d4f1-5250-b171-a7afc4da6d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321818} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4116b2-7d58-5ba6-98c0-9bc6585bd674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321984} {:answer/id #uuid "a62a32b4-fb41-5c95-b967-d406515f0619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322447}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321401} :answer-field/id #uuid "36965466-26eb-5651-b52a-e48d0cb8d65e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "574e3825-54df-5b43-b74f-6544e0543982" :question/math-academy-id "q-36518" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x+4y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3x-y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=0$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+4y)=4 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-y)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."} :step/id #uuid "87e1e468-067a-5b37-817d-ee60c3d401bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a2bcb36-868f-5f54-a6d3-5044da4b78ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409640} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1bdf62-5e9d-55fa-82ff-dab50af9bc96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409643} {:answer/id #uuid "70500de8-c6d4-538a-a490-a042ef069ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409644} {:answer/id #uuid "92afcd1e-56b0-514b-aa96-d6441ab63941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409645} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d893f9-d5e3-5e3f-8ad1-b21394af5064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409646} :answer-field/id #uuid "c178518b-a0fe-5bb1-9ccb-2e562d423be4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "08ef8961-263a-5251-a28f-91bd26031d10" :question/math-academy-id "q-36523" :question/problem "Consider that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first, second and third-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ What is the value of ${f}_{yxy}(1,0)-{f}_{yy}(1,0)-{f}_{xyy}(1,0)$ given that ${f}_{yy}(x,y)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy?$" :question/worked-solution "As the function and its first, second, and third-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ we must have ${f}_{yxy}(x,y)={f}_{xyy}(x,y).$ \n\nTherefore, we can swap the order of the subscripts in the first term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(1,0)-{f}_{yy}(1,0)-{f}_{xyy}(1,0) & ={f}_{xyy}(1,0)-{f}_{yy}(1,0)-{f}_{xyy}(1,0) \\\\  & =\\cancel{{f}_{xyy}(1,0)}-{f}_{yy}(1,0)-\\cancel{{f}_{xyy}(1,0)} \\\\  & =-{f}_{yy}(1,0) \\\\  & =-(1)^{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "95f751f2-5411-50b2-8387-307a1ef77bce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf467131-ed86-5898-9b24-54a81ef3a425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394025} {:answer/id #uuid "408a27ba-3ecd-5304-986f-bfa41f5c422d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394026} {:answer/id #uuid "d277b1a3-8c90-51e0-95ac-2284987b67c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394027} {:answer/id #uuid "4e35c7b0-b843-59cc-b0d5-efe5987a56bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394028} {:answer/id #uuid "b9cd6a57-2825-577b-8bb6-3751ad08ba82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394029}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394029} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c98af2c-655e-5c74-97bc-4c471b7f7278" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "24df999b-6ea6-5095-ada6-d510b0d36076" :question/math-academy-id "q-80652" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=4x+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=4xy-y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}y+2x$" :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-y)=4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{y})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2x+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+2x)=2xy+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."} :step/id #uuid "dd83cd33-8573-5ef5-a0d5-36235eb60d0a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409653}}] :activity/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409637}]}
